    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.surround\n\n### Description\n\nThanks for making this, I use it everyday.\n\nI often like to use [fenced code blocks](https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/#fenced-code-blocks) in markdown. So I might have written something:\n\n\n> Blah blah\n> \n> SOME CODE\n> \n> Blah blah\n\nThen I'll use surround to add a code fence:\n\n> Blah blah\n> \n> \\`\\`\\`\n> &nbsp;&nbsp;SOME CODE\n> \\`\\`\\`\n> Blah blah\n\n...but mini surround will also indent `SOME CODE` with two spaces as you see above, which I don't want. Is there a way to turn off this indentation? I had a look through the config and I couldn't see anything, so I guess this is a feature request.\n\nI know that I could turn off `respect_selection_type` and use:\n\n```\n  respect_selection_type = false,\n  custom_surroundings = {\n    \"¬\" = { left = '```\\n', right = '\\n```' },\n  },\n```\n\n...but I'd like to keep `respect_selection_type=true` if possible.",
