            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I know that I could turn off `respect_selection_type` and use:\n> ...\n> ...but I'd like to keep `respect_selection_type=true` if possible.\n\nYes, unfortunately manually adding new line characters is the recommended approach. Having special behavior for the linewise selection is the whole point of `respect_selection_type` and adding indent feels like a more common use case then not adding it.\n\nOne way to work around is to use charwise selection before applying surrounding. This is possible with setup something like this:\n\n```lua\nrequire('mini.surround').setup({\n  custom_surroundings = {\n    C = { output = { left = '```\\n', right = '\\n```' } },\n  },\n  respect_selection_type = true,\n})\n```\n\nThen instead of something like `vip` do \"put cursor at first column of first line\" - `v` - \"navigate to last column of last line\" - `saC`. If this is a common occurrence, it should be possible to create custom charwise equivalents of common text objects via 'mini.ai'.\n\nCouple of extra notes:\n- `respect_selection_type = false` will also not work 100% with linewise mode here, as it won't work well with indented place in the first place. See #321.\n- If you want that surrounding only in markdown file, create '~/.config/nvim/after/ftplugin/markdown.lua' file and put the following lines in it:\n\n    ```lua\n    vim.b.minisurround_config = {\n      custom_surroundings = {\n        C = { output = { left = '```\\n', right = '\\n```' } },\n      }\n    }\n    ```",
